NNAAMMEE
     ddddbb — kernel debugger

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The ddddbb debugger provides a means for debugging the kernel, and analysing
     the kernel after a system crash ("panic"), with a gdb(1)-like syntax.

     ddddbb is invoked upon a kernel panic when the sysctl(8) _d_d_b_._p_a_n_i_c is set to
     1.  It may be invoked from the console when the sysctl _d_d_b_._c_o_n_s_o_l_e is set
     to 1, using any of the following methods:

        --   Using the key sequence Ctrl-Alt-Esc.

        --   Sending a BREAK when using a serial console.

        --   Writing to the sysctl _d_d_b_._t_r_i_g_g_e_r.

        --   For i386 and amd64 architectures, using the key sequence
            Ctrl-Alt-Delete when the sysctl _m_a_c_h_d_e_p_._k_b_d_r_e_s_e_t is set to 2.

     ddddbb prompts for commands on the console with:

           ddb>

     The general syntax of a ddddbb command is:

           _c_o_m_m_a_n_d [//_m_o_d_i_f_i_e_r_s]   [_a_d_d_r_e_s_s][,,_c_o_u_n_t]

     To save typing, ddddbb makes use of a context inferred from previous com‐
     mands.  In this context, the current location is called _d_o_t.  The
     eexxaammiinnee, sseeaarrcchh, sshhooww ssttrruucctt, and wwrriittee commands update _d_o_t to be that of
     the last address examined or the last location modified, and have intu‐
     itive effects on _n_e_x_t and _p_r_e_v.  All the other commands do not change
     _d_o_t, and set _n_e_x_t to be the same.  (See _V_A_R_I_A_B_L_E_S.)

     An expression can be used in place of _a_d_d_r_e_s_s (see _E_X_P_R_E_S_S_I_O_N_S).  Omit‐
     ting _a_d_d_r_e_s_s in a command uses the last value of _d_o_t.  A missing _c_o_u_n_t is
     taken to be 1 for printing commands or ∞ for stack traces.  Entering a
     blank line causes the last command to be repeated using _n_e_x_t in place of
     _a_d_d_r_e_s_s, a _c_o_u_n_t of 1, and no modifiers.

     ddddbb has a feature like more(1) for the output.  If the number of lines
     output in response to one command exceeds the number set in the _$_l_i_n_e_s
     variable, it displays the message ‘--db_more--’ and waits for a response.

     The valid responses are:

           ⟨ssppaaccee⟩     One more page.
           ⟨rreettuurrnn⟩    One more line.
           qq           Abort the current command, and return to the command
                       input mode.

     The following command line editing keys are provided:

           ^^bb          back one character
           ^^ff          forward one character
           ^^aa          beginning of line
           ^^ee          end of line
           ^^ww          erase word back
           ^^hh | ⟨ddeell⟩  erase previous character
           ^^dd          erase next character
           ^^kk          delete to end of line
           ^^uu          delete line
           ^^pp          previous in command history
           ^^nn          next in command history
           ^^rr          redraw line
           ^^tt          exchange the two characters to the left of the cursor

CCOOMMMMAANNDDSS
     The following commands may be typed at the ‘ddb>’ prompt.  Some commands
     consist of more than one word, and if only the first word or words are
     entered, the possible alternatives to complete the command are displayed
     and no other action is performed.

     hheellpp        List the available commands.

     [ee]xx[aammiinnee] [//bbhhllqqaaAAxxzzoodduurrccssmmiiII] [_a_d_d_r][,,_c_o_u_n_t]
                 Display the contents at address _a_d_d_r according to the formats
                 in the modifier.  If no format is specified, the last formats
                 specified for this command are used.

                 The format characters are:

                 //bb    look at by bytes (8 bits)
                 //hh    look at by half words (16 bits)
                 //ll    look at by long words (32 bits) (default)
                 //qq    look at by long longs (64 bits) (only available on
                       64-bit platforms)
                 //aa    print the location being displayed
                 //AA    print the location with a line number if possible
                 //xx    display in unsigned hex
                 //zz    display in signed hex
                 //oo    display in unsigned octal
                 //dd    display in signed decimal
                 //uu    display in unsigned decimal
                 //rr    display in current radix, signed
                 //cc    display low 8 bits as a character.  Non-printing char‐
                       acters are displayed as an octal escape code (e.g.,
                       '\000').
                 //ss    display the null-terminated string at the location.
                       Non-printing characters are displayed as octal escapes.
                 //mm    display in unsigned hex with character dump at the end
                       of each line.  The location is also displayed in hex at
                       the beginning of each line.
                 //ii    display as an instruction
                 //II    display as an alternate format instruction depending on
                       the machine:

                       alpha     Print affected register contents for every
                                 instruction.
                       amd64,
                       i386      Do not skip padding to the next long word
                                 boundary for unconditional jumps.
                       m88k      Decode instructions for the opposite CPU
                                 model (e.g. m88110 when running on an m88100
                                 processor).

                 The value of _n_e_x_t is set to the _a_d_d_r plus the size of the
                 data examined.

     pp[rriinntt] [//aaxxzzoodduurrcc] [_a_d_d_r]
                 Print _a_d_d_r according to the modifier character.  The valid
                 modifiers are a subset of those from the eexxaammiinnee command, and
                 act as described there.  If no modifier is specified, the
                 last one specified in a previous use of pprriinntt is used.

                 For example,

                       print/x $eax

                 will print something like this:

                       xxxxxx

     pppp[rriinntt] [_a_d_d_r]
                 Pretty-print _a_d_d_r using CTF debug symbols included in the
                 kernel binary image.  The CTF section is normally added by
                 running ctfstrip(1) as part of building a new kernel.

     ww[rriittee] [//bbhhll] [_a_d_d_r] _e_x_p_r [_e_x_p_r _._._.]
                 Write the value of each _e_x_p_r expression at succeeding loca‐
                 tions start at _a_d_d_r.  The write unit size can be specified
                 using one of the modifiers:

                       //bb    byte (8 bits)
                       //hh    half word (16 bits)
                       //ll    long word (32 bits) (default)

                 The value of _n_e_x_t is set to _a_d_d_r plus the size of values
                 written.

                 WWaarrnniinngg: since there is no delimiter between expressions, the
                 command may not parse as you expect.  It is best to enclose
                 each expression in parentheses.

     sseett $$_n_a_m_e [==] _e_x_p_r
                 Set the named variable or register with the value of _e_x_p_r.
                 For valid variable names, see _V_A_R_I_A_B_L_E_S.

     bboooott _h_o_w    Reboot the machine depending on _h_o_w:

                 bboooott ssyynncc      Sync disks and reboot.
                 bboooott ccrraasshh     Dump core and reboot.
                 bboooott dduummpp      Sync disks, dump core and reboot.
                 bboooott hhaalltt      Just halt.
                 bboooott rreebboooott    Just reboot.
                 bboooott ppoowweerrooffff  Power down the machine whenever possible; if
                                it fails, just halt.

     bbrreeaakk [_a_d_d_r][,,_c_o_u_n_t]
                 Set a break point at _a_d_d_r.  If _c_o_u_n_t is supplied, ddddbb allows
                 the breakpoint to be silently hit (_c_o_u_n_t - 1) times before
                 stopping at the break point.

     dd[eelleettee] [_a_d_d_r]
                 Delete the break point set with the bbrreeaakk command.

     ss[tteepp] [//pp] [,,_c_o_u_n_t]
                 Single step _c_o_u_n_t times.  If the //pp modifier is specified,
                 print each instruction at each step.  Otherwise, only print
                 the last instruction.

                 WWaarrnniinngg: depending on machine type, it may not be possible to
                 single-step through some low-level code paths.  On machines
                 with software-emulated single-stepping (e.g., alpha), step‐
                 ping through code executed by interrupt handlers will proba‐
                 bly do the wrong thing.

     ccaallll _n_a_m_e((_e_x_p_r [,, _e_x_p_r _._._.] ))
                 Call the function named by _n_a_m_e with the argument(s) listed
                 in parentheses.  Parentheses may be omitted if the function
                 takes no arguments.  The number of arguments is currently
                 limited to 10.

     cc[oonnttiinnuuee] [//cc]
                 Continue execution until a breakpoint or watchpoint.  If the
                 //cc modifier is given, instructions are counted while execut‐
                 ing.

                 WWaarrnniinngg: when counting with //cc, ddddbb is really silently sin‐
                 gle-stepping.  This means that single-stepping on low-level
                 code may cause strange behavior.

     wwaattcchh _a_d_d_r [,,_s_i_z_e]
                 Set a watchpoint for the region starting at _a_d_d_r.  Execution
                 stops and control returns to ddddbb when an attempt is made to
                 modify a watched region.  The _s_i_z_e argument defaults to 4.

                 If you specify a wrong space address, the request is rejected
                 with an error message.

                 WWaarrnniinngg: attempts to watch wired kernel memory may cause an
                 unrecoverable error on some systems (e.g., i386).

     ddwwaattcchh _a_d_d_r
                 Delete the watchpoint at address _a_d_d_r that was previously set
                 with a wwaattcchh command.

     hhaannggmmaann [//ss[00--99]]
                 This is a tiny and handy tool for random kernel hangs analy‐
                 sis, of which its depth is controlled by the optional argu‐
                 ment of the default value of five.  It uses some sophisti‐
                 cated heuristics to spot the global symbol that caused the
                 hang.  Since the discovering algorithm is a probabilistic
                 one, you may spend substantial time to figure the exact sym‐
                 bol name.  This smart thing requires a little of your atten‐
                 tion, the input it accepts is mostly of the same format as
                 that of the famous hangman(6) game, to which it, apparently,
                 is obliged by the name.  Hint: the nm(1) utility might help.

     uunnttiill [//pp]  Stop at the next "call" or "return" instruction.  If the //pp
                 modifier is specified, ddddbb prints the call nesting depth and
                 the cumulative instruction count at each call or return.
                 Otherwise, it stays silent until the matching return is hit.

     mmaattcchh [//pp]  Stop at the next matching return instruction.  If the //pp mod‐
                 ifier is specified, ddddbb prints the call nesting depth and the
                 cumulative instruction count at each call or return.  Other‐
                 wise, it remains mostly quiet.

     nneexxtt [//pp]   The nneexxtt command is a synonym for mmaattcchh.

     kkiillll _p_i_d    Send an uncatchable SIGABRT signal to the process specified
                 by the _p_i_d argument.

     ttrraaccee [//ttuu] [_f_r_a_m_e_a_d_d_r][,,_c_o_u_n_t]
                 Show the stack trace.  The //tt modifier interprets the
                 _f_r_a_m_e_a_d_d_r argument as the TID of a process and shows the
                 stack trace of that process.  _f_r_a_m_e_a_d_d_r is subject to the
                 radix; use the 0t prefix to enter a decimal TID.  The //tt mod‐
                 ifier is not supported on all platforms.  The //uu modifier
                 shows the stack trace of user space; if omitted, the kernel
                 stack is traced instead.  The _c_o_u_n_t argument is the limit on
                 the number of frames to be followed.  If _c_o_u_n_t is omitted,
                 all frames are printed.

                 WWaarrnniinngg: user space stack trace is valid only if the machine
                 dependent code supports it.

     sseeaarrcchh [//bbhhll] [_a_d_d_r] _v_a_l_u_e [_m_a_s_k] [,,_c_o_u_n_t]
                 Search memory for a value beginning at _a_d_d_r.  This command
                 might fail in interesting ways if it doesn't find the
                 searched-for value.  This is because ddddbb doesn't always re‐
                 cover from touching bad memory.  The optional _c_o_u_n_t argument
                 limits the search.  The modifiers are the same as those of
                 the wwrriittee command.

                 The _n_e_x_t address is set to the address where _v_a_l_u_e is found,
                 or just after where the search area finishes.

     rreebboooott      Shortcut for bboooott rreebboooott

     sshhooww _w_h_a_t   Displays various things, depending on _w_h_a_t:

                 sshhooww bbccssttaattss
                       Prints the buffer cache statistics.

                 sshhooww bbrreeaakkss
                       Prints a list of all breakpoints that have been set
                       with the bbrreeaakk command.

                 sshhooww bbuuff _a_d_d_r
                       Prints the _s_t_r_u_c_t _b_u_f at _a_d_d_r.

                 sshhooww eexxtteennttss
                       Prints a detailed list of all extents.

                 sshhooww lloocckkss [_a_d_d_r]
                       Prints the list of locks held by a thread.  If an op‐
                       tional address is not specified, curproc is assumed.
                       The ooppttiioonn WWIITTNNEESSSS is required for this command to be
                       available.

                 sshhooww mmaalllloocc [_a_d_d_r]
                       Prints malloc debugging information if available.  If
                       an optional address is specified, only information
                       about that address is printed.

                 sshhooww mmaapp [//ff] _a_d_d_r
                       Prints the vm_map at _a_d_d_r.  If the //ff modifier is spec‐
                       ified, the complete map is printed.

                 sshhooww mmbbuuff [//ccpp] _a_d_d_r
                       Prints the _s_t_r_u_c_t _m_b_u_f header at _a_d_d_r.  Depending on
                       the mbuf flags _s_t_r_u_c_t _p_k_t_h_d_r and _s_t_r_u_c_t _m___e_x_t are
                       printed as well.  If the //cc modifier is specified,
                       print the mbuf chain linked with the m_next pointer.
                       //pp does the same using m_nextpkt.  Both can be com‐
                       bined.

                 sshhooww mmoouunntt [//ff] _a_d_d_r
                       Prints the _s_t_r_u_c_t _m_o_u_n_t at _a_d_d_r.  If the //ff modifier is
                       specified, prints out all vnodes (see also sshhooww vvnnooddee)
                       and also all bufs (see also sshhooww bbuuff) on all those vn‐
                       odes.

                 sshhooww nnffssnnooddee [//ff] _a_d_d_r
                       Prints the _s_t_r_u_c_t _n_f_s_n_o_d_e at _a_d_d_r.  If the //ff modifier
                       is specified, prints out additional information as
                       well.

                 sshhooww nnffssrreeqq [//ff] _a_d_d_r
                       Prints the _s_t_r_u_c_t _n_f_s_r_e_q at _a_d_d_r.  If the //ff modifier
                       is specified, prints out additional information as
